            <ArticleTitle>Perspectives of a tailored lifestyle program for people with severe mental illness receiving housing support.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/ppc.12239</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The purpose of the present study was to describe the acceptability of the lifestyle program PHYS/CAT and to get information about the tools used for assessment of functional exercise capacity, cognitive performance, and self-health-related quality of life.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN AND METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">The findings are based on focus groups and the researchers' experiences of conducting the program as well as using the assessment tools.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The acceptability of the program and the assessment tools was mainly satisfactory.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PRACTICE IMPLICATIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">The program with relational, educational, and supportive dimensions may be a promising tool to be integrated into daily nursing care.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© 2017 Wiley Periodicals, Inc.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
